\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Linear Gaussian SSM: state RMSE (lower is better). $x_t=0.8x_{t-1}+0.5\varepsilon _t$, $y_t=x_t+0.8\eta _t$. 50 steps, resampling when ESS $<0.5N$; mean $\pm $ 95\% CI over 10 observation sequences (100 filter runs; 10 for OT and Gumbel).}}{1}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:linear-rmse}{{1}{1}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {(a)} The values of Table~\ref {tab:linear-rmse} against $N$ (log scale), one line per row. Colour is the method family: black DSR, gray classical, blue moment matching, orange soft/Gumbel, green optimal transport, purple variational; line style and marker separate rows within a family. Whiskers are the table's 95\% CIs; points are shifted sideways so whiskers do not overlap. \textbf  {(b)} Each method minus systematic resampling, computed per observation sequence and then averaged (mean $\pm $ paired 95\% $t$ interval over the 10 sequences). Filled markers: the interval excludes 0. The table's $\pm $ for systematic, printed under each $N$, is dominated by how hard each sequence is, which every method shares; pairing removes that, so differences invisible in (a) can be resolved here. \textbf  {(c)} Mean squared state error, split into the exact Kalman filter's error (gray; it depends only on the data) and the extra error from the particle filter (colour; the squared posterior-mean RMSE of Table~\ref {tab:linear-posterior_mean_rmse}). The particle filter contributes only 2--9\% of the MSE, and RMSE adds the two in quadrature, so the state RMSE cannot separate resamplers; compare methods with (b) or with Table~\ref {tab:linear-posterior_mean_rmse}.}}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:linear-rmse}{{1}{2}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Linear Gaussian SSM: posterior-mean RMSE vs.\ exact Kalman filter (lower is better). $x_t=0.8x_{t-1}+0.5\varepsilon _t$, $y_t=x_t+0.8\eta _t$. 50 steps, resampling when ESS $<0.5N$; mean $\pm $ 95\% CI over 10 observation sequences (100 filter runs; 10 for OT and Gumbel).}}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
